EXEC_OPS()
	Traverse all tokens
		1. IF i-th token is operand-program
			1.1. IF the next (i-th + 1) token is &&, || or ')'
				1.1.1. Expand and assign variables and expand wildcards in the current token
				1.1.2. Update redirections (after path and variable expansion new redirection-operands may appear)
				1.1.3. Determine program's name and its arguments
				1.1.4. IF program does not have any arguments (means it consists entirely of redirections or variable assignments or mixed redirections and assignments)
					1.1.4.1. Traverse all redirections creating/truncating files (redirections' paths/operands); Or error we return the corresponding code
					1.1.4.2. Copy all local variables created inside this operand (`my_env` field of `t_operand` structure) into the current session's environment
				1.1.5. IF program does have arguments
					1.1.5.1. IF program name argv[0] is a 'regular built-in' (cd, pwd, export, unset, env, exit)
						1.1.5.2. Execute this built-in in the current process (without fork())
						1.1.5.2. Traverse all redirections creating/truncating files (redirections' paths/operands); Or error we return the corresponding code
						(actually we ignore all redirections here for the sake of simplicity, but we create/truncate files when necessary)
						1.1.5.3. Update $?
					1.1.5.2. ELSE (program name is not a built-in or the 'special built-in' echo that actually has full-fledged redirections support)
						1.1.5.2.1. IF program name is 'echo'
							1.1.5.2.1.1. Just leave program name as it is
						1.1.5.2.2. ELSE
							1.1.5.2.2.1. Search for a program with this name in PATH
							1.1.5.2.2.2. IF program was found
								1.1.5.2.2.2.1. Update argv[0] with path to program (from PATH)
							1.1.5.2.2.3. ELSE
								1.1.5.2.2.3.1. Leave program name as it is
					1.1.5.3. Do fork()
						1.1.5.3.1. IN CHILD: Assign all redirections and heredocs
						1.1.5.3.2. IN CHILD: IF program name is 'echo'
							1.1.5.3.2.1. Execute echo built-in
						1.1.5.3.3. IN CHILD: ELSE
							1.1.5.3.3.1. IN CHILD: Form environment for this program (just copy all variables that have ENV type into new array and add `my_env` to this array)
							1.1.5.3.3.2. IN CHILD: Do execve() passing to it the new environment array and the arguments
					1.1.5.4. Wait unil program is finished
					1.1.5.5. Get this program's returned value and update $?
					1.1.5.6. IF the program's returned value is SUCCESS
						1.1.5.6.1. IF the next (i-th + 1) token is && (and returned value is SUCCESS)
							1.1.5.6.1.1. IF the next (i-th + 2) token after && is operand-program
								1.1.5.6.1.1.1. Move to this token
								1.1.5.6.1.1.2. Go to step 1.1.1.
							1.1.5.6.1.2. IF the next (i-th + 2) token after && is '('
								1.1.5.6.1.2.1. Launch a subshell
								1.1.5.6.1.2.2. Close all FDs inherited from parent
								1.1.5.6.1.2.3. Call EXEC_OPS() in the subshell
								1.1.5.6.1.2.4. Get returned value of the last command executed in the subshell
						1.1.5.6.2. IF the next token (i-th + 1) is || (and returned value is SUCCESS)
							1.1.5.6.2.1. IF the next (i-th + 2) token after || is operand-program
								1.1.5.6.2.1.1. Skip this (i-th + 1) || and this next (i-th + 2) operand
							1.1.5.6.2.2. IF the next (i-th + 2) token after || is '('
								1.1.5.6.2.2.1. Skip all inside this parenthesis block
								1.1.5.6.2.2.2. Continue		
						1.1.5.6.3. IF the next token (i-th + 1) is ')'
							1.1.5.6.3.1. Exit the current subshell
					1.1.5.7. IF the program's returned value is FAILURE
						1.1.5.7.1. IF the next (i-th + 1) token is && (and returned value is FAILURE)
							1.1.5.7.1.1. IF the next (i-th + 2) token after && is operand-program
								1.1.5.7.1.1.1. Skip this (i-th + 1) && and this next (i-th + 2) operand
								1.1.5.7.1.1.2. Continue
							1.1.5.7.1.2. IF the next (i-th + 2) token after && is '('
								1.1.5.7.1.2.1. Skip all inside this parenthesis block
								1.1.5.7.1.2.2. Continue
						1.1.5.7.2. IF the next (i-th + 1) token is || (and returned value is FAILURE)
							1.1.5.7.2.1. IF the next (i-th + 2) token after || is operand-program
								1.1.5.7.2.1.1. Move to this token
								1.1.5.7.2.1.2. Go to step 1.1.1.
							1.1.5.7.2.2. IF the next (i-th + 2) token after || is '('
								1.1.5.7.2.2.1. Launch a subshell
								1.1.5.7.2.2.2. Close all FDs inherited from parent
								1.1.5.7.2.2.3. Call EXEC_OPS() in the subshell
								1.1.5.7.2.2.4. Get returned value of the last command executed in the subshell
						1.1.5.7.3. IF the next token (i-th + 1) is ')'
							1.1.5.7.3.1. Exit the current subshell

			1.2. IF the next (i-th + 1) token is | (we have to reach the end of this pipeline meanwhile connecting its components together via pipes)
				1.2.1. Go one token forward
				1.2.2. LOOP until we reach the end of tokens' array
					1.2.2.1. IF the current token is |
						1.2.2.1.1. Create a new pipe using pipe()
						1.2.2.1.2. Add created pipe in the array of all pipes
						1.2.2.1.3. IF this is the first pipe in the current pipeline
							1.2.2.1.3.1. Assign to 'STDIN' (read-end) of the last operand-program found the NONE_PIPE value (-1)
						1.2.2.1.4. ELSE (not the first pipe)
							1.2.2.1.4.1. Assign to 'STDIN' (read-end) of the last operand-program found the index of the second to last pipe in pipes array
						1.2.2.1.5. Assign to 'STDOUT' (write-end) of the last operand-program the index of the last pipe created
					1.2.2.2. ELSE IF the current token is an operand-program
						1.2.2.2.1. Mark this operand-program as the last operand-program found
					1.2.2.3. ELSE IF the current token is a logical operator (this means we reached the end of the current pipeline)
						1.2.2.3.1. Assign to 'STDOUT' (write-end) of the last operand-program found the NONE_PIPE value (-1)
						1.2.2.3.2. Remember the index of the last token in the pipeline
						1.2.2.3.3. Break
					1.2.2.4. ELSE IF the current token is '(' (in this case it's guaranteed the previous token was a pipe)
						1.2.2.4.1. Assign to 'STDIN' (read-end) of this opening-parenthesis the index of the last pipe found
						1.2.2.4.2. Skip this entire parenthesis block and move ourselves onto the first token that goes after ')'
						1.2.2.4.3. IF there is no token after ')' (it mean the ')' was the last token) OR after ')' goes a logical operator
							1.2.2.4.3.1. Assign to 'STDOUT' (write-end) of this opening-parentheses the NONE_PIPE value (-1)
						1.2.2.4.4. ELSE (not the last token; in fact after ')' may go only logical operator or a pipe)
							1.2.2.4.4.1. Do nothing? (HERE I'M NOT SURE WHAT TO DO)
					1.2.2.5. Go to the next token
				1.2.3. END of LOOP until we reach the end of tokens' array
			
			# We're on the i-th token (where our pipeline begins). Now we're gonna execute all operand-programs in this pipeline
			1.3. LOOP until we reach the last token in the current pipeline
				1.3.1. IF the current token is |
					1.3.1.1. Skip it
				1.3.2. IF the current token is '('
					1.3.2.1. Launch a subshell
					1.3.2.2. Close all FDs inherited from parent
					1.3.2.3. Call EXEC_OPS() in the subshell
					1.3.2.4. Get returned value of the last command executed in the subshell
				1.3.3. IF the current token is ')'
					1.3.3.1. Exit the current subshell
				1.3.4. IF the current token is an operand-program
					1.3.4.1. Do the same actions as we do in steps 1.1.1 - 1.1.5.5., i.e. expand everything, form arguments, search in PATH, fork() and then execve() and etc.
				1.3.5. Next token
			1.4. END of LOOP until we reach the last token in the current pipeline
			1.5. Wait for all launched operand-programs in the processed pipeline
			1.6. Get the returned code of the last operand-program executed in the processed pipeline

		2. IF i-th token is a '('
			2.1. Launch a subshell
			2.2. Close all FDs inherited from parent
			2.3. Call EXEC_OPS() in the subshell
			2.4. Get returned value of the last command executed in the subshell

		3. Next token	

	End of tokens traversal

END of EXEC_OPS()
